# Full-stack example environment template. Copy it to a private path OUTSIDE the checkout,
# fill every empty value, keep it mode 0600, and pass it with `docker compose --env-file`.
# Empty is a deliberate value here: every variable the manifests require uses ${VAR:?}, so an
# unfilled install fails at `config` time instead of starting something unspecified.
# Random values below are generated, never committed:
#   token    -> python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"   (>= 24 chars)
#   UUID     -> python3 -c "import uuid; print(uuid.uuid4())"

# --- Images ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Third-party images: repository@sha256:<64 lowercase hex>. Nothing here is published by this
# project; resolve each digest for linux/amd64 yourself. Candidates:
# components/data/store-signoz/versions.json and components/control/dagu/CONTRACT.md.
LO_CLICKHOUSE_IMAGE=
LO_ZOOKEEPER_IMAGE=
LO_SIGNOZ_IMAGE=
LO_SIGNOZ_COLLECTOR_IMAGE=
# OpenTelemetry Collector contrib image, used by the front door and the Linux agent alike.
LO_OTEL_IMAGE=
# Dagu engine image (the runner that executes the job definitions in ./dags).
LO_DAGU_IMAGE=
# Healthchecks image (BSD-3-Clause): the deadline owner for "the job did not run", and the check the
# independent platform witness pings. Pin the linux/amd64 digest recorded in
# components/control/job-observe/versions.json -- the tag is multi-arch and the manifest sets
# platform: linux/amd64, so a digest pulled for another architecture stops the container.
LO_HEALTHCHECKS_IMAGE=
# Operator portal image: Homepage, which renders the config local_observe/platform/homepage.py
# produces. The digest this repository resolved and saw started is in
# components/control/homepage/versions.json; resolve it for your own platform before filling this in,
# and keep tag and digest together in one value (the manifest sets pull_policy: never, so the image
# must already be in the local daemon).
LO_HOMEPAGE_IMAGE=
# First-party images: build them from this checkout (docs/BUILD.md). Both manifests set
# pull_policy: never, so the value must already exist in the local daemon -- the image config
# ID printed by `docker image inspect --format '{{.Id}}' <name>` (see docs/BUILD.md, which
# records the ID plus the source revision it came from and notes it is not a registry digest).
LO_PLATFORM_IMAGE=
LO_INVENTORY_IMAGE=

# --- Store user-defined function binary ------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute path to a separately downloaded, SHA256-verified linux-amd64 histogramQuantile
# binary, and the checksum OF THAT BINARY (not of the archive it arrived in).
LO_HISTOGRAM_BINARY=
LO_HISTOGRAM_SHA256=

# --- Identity of the host the agent observes -------------------------------------------------
# Any name for this synthetic host; it becomes the host.name resource attribute.
LO_HOST_NAME=host-01
# Generate one UUID per installation and write the SAME value into
# inventory/declared.yaml as the host resource id: the Sigma runner refuses to evaluate a
# resource it cannot resolve in the inventory ("Undeclared resource").
LO_RESOURCE_ID=

# --- Read-only mounts the Linux agent needs --------------------------------------------------
# Absolute root of the Linux host to observe; "/" is a broad read and needs a deliberate yes.
# Never point this at a Windows root.
LO_HOST_ROOT=/
# Absolute directory holding the approved .log files the agent may tail; create it empty.
LO_LOG_DIR=
# Regular expression selecting which filesystems the agent reports. .* covers every mount.
LO_FILESYSTEM_MOUNT_PATTERN=.*

# --- Credentials ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every line below names a FILE holding one credential, never the credential itself. Each manifest
# mounts its file into the container as a Compose secret, so no token is ever an environment value
# -- an env value is readable by anyone who can run `docker inspect` or read /proc/<pid>/environ.
# Create them all the same way, into a directory only you can read:
#   mkdir -p -m 700 /approved/deploy/full/private
#   umask 077; printf '%s' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" > /approved/deploy/full/private/ingest-token
# Use printf, not echo: the collector reads these files verbatim, so a trailing newline would be
# sent inside the Authorization header and every request would be refused. Generate each value
# separately -- reusing one across roles defeats the role model, and platform tokens must be 24
# characters or longer, which the platform checks before it serves a single request.
# Bearer token accepted by the front door from producers (mounted in the front door and, as the
# same value, in the Linux agent that pushes to it).
LO_INGEST_TOKEN_FILE=
# Bearer token accepted by the store collector from the front door only -- a distinct secret.
LO_STORE_TOKEN_FILE=
# SigNoz session-signing secret. Still an environment value: it belongs to an image this repository
# does not build, and the exception is recorded in components/data/store-signoz/CONTRACT.md.
SIGNOZ_JWT_SECRET=
# Bearer token accepted by the inventory reader API.
LO_INVENTORY_TOKEN_FILE=
# Absolute path to the Dagu config file that holds the runner's basic-auth password. Dagu takes
# this credential from a config file only, so the file is a rendered fragment, not a bare password:
#   umask 077; printf 'auth:\n  basic:\n    password: "%s"\n' "$password" \
#     > /approved/deploy/full/private/dagu-config.yaml
# The username (lo-runner) and auth mode stay in the manifest; Dagu 2.16.2 also binds
# DAGU_AUTH_BASIC_PASSWORD, which the manifest deliberately does not set -- see its compose file.
LO_DAGU_CONFIG_FILE=
# Fresh operator setup selects this username; the default preserves existing deployments.
LO_DAGU_USERNAME=lo-runner
# Optional Operations password hash file inside the existing /config mount.
# Set /config/operator-account.json after installing the setup-generated account there.
LO_OPERATOR_ACCOUNT_FILE=
# Absolute path to the platform role credentials file: a JSON list of
# {identity, role, token} objects, roles reader/producer/proposer/human/executor/summary.
# Mounted as a Compose secret, never passed as an environment value. examples/full/README.md
# writes one; keep it mode 0600.
LO_PLATFORM_CREDENTIALS_FILE=
# The portal's credential is the `summary` row of that same file, copied into its own one-value file:
# the platform restricts that role to /v1/me and /v1/overview, and Homepage substitutes this file's
# contents into each read widget's Authorization header, so the token appears in no YAML and no HTML.
# Write it with printf and no trailing newline, then chmod 0444 so uid 65532 can read the mount.
#   umask 077; printf '%s' "$summary_token" > /approved/deploy/full/private/homepage-overview-token
# Reusing a reader or human token here is how a portal becomes an operator's authority: it needs none.
LO_HOMEPAGE_TOKEN_FILE=
# Healthchecks' Django session-signing key: one random value, no trailing newline (printf, never
# echo). Upstream reads it from a file natively (its settings helper prefers SECRET_KEY_FILE over
# SECRET_KEY), so this is the same secret files shape as every other credential here -- a path in the
# environment, the value under /run/secrets/. It is NOT the console login: Healthchecks ships no
# admin password at all, because the account is created against the running container
# (`manage.py createsuperuser`, README of this example step 5b) and a password would otherwise be an
# environment value in every resolved model. Until that command has run, the console has no login.
#   umask 077; printf '%s' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" > /approved/deploy/full/private/healthchecks-secret
LO_HEALTHCHECKS_SECRET_FILE=
# The producer-role token from that same file, in its own one-value file: the Sigma runner posts
# intake events with it. A producer only ever submits observations; it cannot approve or run
# anything. Copy the row's token out, do not generate a second one -- the platform authenticates
# the runner against the credentials file it already has.
LO_PRODUCER_TOKEN_FILE=

# Optional, and off by default: the MCP tool surface's per-agent credentials (chat integration).
# No service in THIS example declares an MCP reader, so this line documents the switch an operator adds
# to the process they run: the source-tree form (`python -m uvicorn
# local_observe.platform.mcp:app_factory`) or the packaged one (`components/control/mcp/compose.yaml`,
# added as one `include:` entry — the "Optional: the MCP tool surface" block near the foot of this file is
# that opt-in). The value is a CONTAINER path inside that process: stage the file in
# the directory LO_PLATFORM_POLICY_DIR already mounts read-only at /config — beside actions.json — and
# name it from inside there, exactly as LO_NOTIFY_CHANNELS does. The document is a JSON list of 1 to 32
# rows, one per agent, each naming EXACTLY these four keys — a fifth key is a boot refusal, because a key
# the code does not read is a key the operator believes is configured — and it holds every secret the
# surface has (at most 16 KiB of it):
#   [{"identity": "reader-1", "role": "reader",   "bearer_token": "<24+ chars, unique>",
#     "platform_token": "<a reader row of LO_PLATFORM_CREDENTIALS_FILE>"},
#    {"identity": "runner-1", "role": "proposer", "bearer_token": "<a different value>",
#     "platform_token": "<a proposer row of that same file>"}]
# A platform_token the platform does not know is not caught here: the map is validated on its own, and
# what that agent gets is a 401 on its first request.
# Keep it mode 0600 and never world-readable: a bearer credential that any local uid can read is a
# credential any local uid holds. Roles come from the platform's own vocabulary (reader, proposer,
# executor); `human`, `producer` and `summary` are refused at boot, because an MCP agent is not a
# person, does not sign observations, and must not hold the portal's read of /v1/overview. Two rules
# stop the boot rather than degrade: this variable and LO_MCP_TOKEN_FILE may not both be set, and a
# mounted map that is malformed, duplicated or names a refused role is fatal.
# Unset or blank, the surface keeps the v0.1 shape: one shared reader token in LO_MCP_TOKEN_FILE, and
# the read-only tools only — which is what the platform ships today, so nothing here breaks an
# installation that never reads this line.
# LO_MCP_IDENTITIES=/config/mcp-identities.json

# --- Built artefacts the control plane reads -------------------------------------------------
# Absolute directory holding inventory.db, built by `lo-inventory build` (see README.md).
LO_INVENTORY_SNAPSHOT_DIR=
# Same directory again: the platform reads the identical snapshot, mounted read-only at
# /inventory. Point both variables at one directory, never at two copies.
LO_PLATFORM_INDEX_DIR=
# Absolute directory containing the action policy file actions.json (the platform reads
# /config/actions.json from it). examples/platform/actions.json is the starting point.
LO_PLATFORM_POLICY_DIR=
# Absolute path to ONE compiled Sigma rule artifact, e.g.
# /approved/path/to/local-observe/examples/sigma/compiled/process-marker.json
# produced by the pinned compiler (components/control/sigma/compiler.lock). Copy it out of the
# checkout first: the artifact names the rule revision it was compiled from.
LO_SIGMA_ARTIFACT_FILE=
# Absolute directory holding the .yaml job definitions Dagu may execute. Create it EMPTY and copy
# in only the job files you have reviewed -- Dagu executes the shell commands a DAG names, so a DAG
# nobody read is a command line nobody read, and the UI cannot add one because the bind is
# read-only. The directory must exist before `up`: the manifest sets `create_host_path: false`, so
# a missing path stops the boot instead of mounting an empty directory Docker made for you.
# Dagu reads it as uid 1000, so it and its files need read permission for that uid.
#   mkdir -p -m 755 /approved/deploy/full/dags
#   install -m 644 examples/jobs/inspect-synthetic.yaml /approved/deploy/full/dags/
LO_DAGU_DAGS_DIR=
# Absolute directory holding the rendered Homepage config (services.yaml, settings.yaml and the
# others local_observe/platform/homepage.py returns, plus custom.css copied from
# local_observe/platform/static/homepage.css and an empty custom.js). README.md step 5 renders it; the
# bind is read-only with create_host_path: false, so the directory must exist and be non-empty before
# `up` -- an empty one gives a portal with no tiles and no warning.
#   mkdir -p -m 755 /approved/deploy/full/portal-config
LO_HOMEPAGE_CONFIG_DIR=
# Absolute path to the deployed copy of scripts/homepage_start.cjs, mounted at /bootstrap.cjs. It is
# product source and this component ships no second copy, because the running upgrade rehearsal hashes
# the bootstrap inside the container against the source-tree file it shipped. Copy it into the deploy
# tree with the rest of the source and point this at where it landed; it must be world-readable (0644),
# since uid 65532 executes it.
LO_HOMEPAGE_BOOTSTRAP=

# --- LAN synthetics (component `synthetics`, synthetics component) ---------------------------------------------
# Gatus probes declared services and this product's adapter turns its results into availability and
# coverage findings. Neither service publishes a port: the detector reaches the engine over this
# project's network, and Gatus's status page is deliberately not exposed (see
# components/control/synthetics/CONTRACT.md, "Publication"). Deleting the include entry is supported
# and costs exactly the row's `If disabled` clause -- no synthetic coverage; every rule the detector
# used to evaluate opens `coverage` rather than resolving.
# Engine image. Read components/control/synthetics/versions.json first: it names the release tag the
# digest belongs to (v5.36.0), the registry URL and date it was read from, the index digest of the same
# release, and the moving-channel digest the component supersedes and why. The manifest states
# `platform: linux/amd64`, so the value must be that architecture's digest, and `pull_policy: never`
# means it has to be in the local daemon before `up`.
LO_GATUS_IMAGE=
# Absolute path to YOUR rendered copy of components/control/synthetics/config.yaml. Copy the shipped
# shape out first -- the bind is read-only with create_host_path: false, so a path that does not exist
# stops `config` instead of mounting an empty file the engine would reject. Two lines in that document
# are marked RENDER-ME (the basic-auth username and the bcrypt hash); leave them unrendered and the
# engine does not start at all -- it panics decoding the hash field, which is fail-closed but also a
# crash loop, so render both and restart. The rendered copy then holds a credential (the bcrypt hash),
# so keep it 0600.
#   mkdir -p -m 700 /approved/deploy/full/gatus
#   install -m 600 components/control/synthetics/config.yaml /approved/deploy/full/gatus/config.yaml
LO_GATUS_CONFIG=
# The credential the detector presents to the engine: base64("<user>:<password>"), NOT a bearer token.
# The pinned Gatus release offers only HTTP Basic (or OIDC) on the status route, and its middleware
# refuses any Authorization header that does not begin `basic ` -- the option names, the route
# ordering and the generator are in components/control/synthetics/CONTRACT.md. The user half must equal
# security.basic.username in the file above, and the password half must be the one whose bcrypt hash is
# in it, so three values in two files have to agree; get it wrong and the answer is a 401 the detector
# reports as coverage, which is the loud direction.
#   user=lo-detector; pass=$(openssl rand -hex 24)
#   umask 077; printf '%s' "$(printf '%s:%s' "$user" "$pass" | base64 -w0)" \
#     > /approved/deploy/full/private/gatus-token
#   chmod 0444 /approved/deploy/full/private/gatus-token        # Docker mounts the host mode
LO_GATUS_TOKEN_FILE=
# Absolute directory holding ONE detection rule document per monitored target, mounted read-only at
# /rules. The detector reads /rules/availability.yaml, so that filename is not free, and one detector
# process evaluates one rule -- a second target is a second detector service in an overlay, not a second
# entry in this file (the limitation is named in docs/COMPONENTS.md's synthetics row). The rule's
# `resource_id` must be a declared inventory UUID (step 3) or the tick is refused; shape:
#   examples/platform/availability.yaml -- copy it out and edit it, and use its default
#   max_age_seconds (120), not the stage's 30: the product's Gatus config probes every 30 seconds, and
#   a freshness bound tighter than the probe interval flaps between two results.
#   mkdir -p -m 755 /approved/deploy/full/rules
#   install -m 644 examples/platform/availability.yaml /approved/deploy/full/rules/availability.yaml
LO_DETECTION_RULE_DIR=
# The credential the detector presents to platform intake. It is its OWN file, and the token inside it
# is the THIRD `producer` row of the role file step 4 writes
# (/approved/deploy/full/private/platform-credentials.json) — after the Sigma runner's and the anomaly
# producer's, and staged the same way as those two (see LO_ANOMALY_PRODUCER_TOKEN_FILE further down).
# Why a third and not a copy: one row of that file carries exactly one `identity`, and
# `local_observe/platform/state.py` refuses an event whose `source` is not the identity its token
# authenticated as — `Source identity differs from authenticated producer` — so one token cannot be two
# producers .
# Where this identity lives is the difference from the two rows above: the detector has NO
# `LO_*_SOURCE` variable, because its identity is the `source:` line of the rule document it evaluates —
# a value in LO_DETECTION_RULE_DIR above, not in this file. So the row's `identity` must equal the
# `source` EVERY rule document in that directory names (the example installed above reads
# `source: stage-detector`, so an unedited copy means that name here); a rule naming a different source
# is a fourth row and a fourth file, and a rule naming one this token is not is refused forever. What
# that failure looks like, since it is not `docker ps`: the detector's evidence POST is accepted
# (`Store.put_evidence` keys the row by the authenticated identity and reads no `source`), its event POST
# is refused, the pending batch is retried every 2 s, and because a retained batch never rewrites the
# cursor, the staleness healthcheck goes `unhealthy` about max(3 x window, 60) s later while the log
# still names only `TransportError` at WARNING (the refusal sentence is DEBUG-only). Mint the row rather
# than copying the sigma one — the store dedups on (source, source_event_id), so two producers sharing
# one identity fold two verdict streams into one and lose a verdict.
#   {"identity": "stage-detector", "role": "producer", "token": "<24+ chars, its own value>"}
#   umask 077; printf '%s' "<that row's token>" \
#     > /approved/deploy/full/private/detector-producer-token
#   chmod 0444 /approved/deploy/full/private/detector-producer-token   # Docker mounts the host mode
# Unset here, `docker compose config` stops and names this variable: the intended behaviour of a
# required value. This repository builds no image and starts no container, so neither that refusal nor
# the working path has been run from it.
LO_DETECTOR_PRODUCER_TOKEN_FILE=
# Optional. Which Gatus endpoint the detector reads, as its DERIVED key (group + name, sanitised and
# joined with "_" -- read it back from /api/v1/endpoints/statuses, never guess it). The manifest
# default is the endpoint the shipped config declares, so this line is only here for an overlay that
# renames it: LO_GATUS_URL=http://gatus:8080/api/v1/endpoints/lo_platform-http/statuses
# Optional. Evaluation cadence in seconds, 5..3600; the default below is what the platform stage has
# always run at. It is also the base of the detector's own staleness bound (max(3 x window, 60)), which
# the container healthcheck recomputes from this same value at run time. A value out of range, or not
# an integer, stops the detector rather than being ignored.
LO_DETECTION_WINDOW_SECONDS=5

# --- Sigma target and endpoints --------------------------------------------------------------
# Producer identity the runner stamps on its events; it must match the `identity` of the
# producer credential in LO_PLATFORM_CREDENTIALS_FILE, since the platform records who said it.
LO_SIGMA_SOURCE=sigma-example
# The Sigma runner queries the store directly over the project network and posts to the
# platform over the same network. Both are plaintext HTTP inside this project, which the code
# only accepts while LO_INTERNAL_ALLOW_HTTP=1; set it to 0 and use HTTPS once they are TLS.
LO_CLICKHOUSE_URL=http://clickhouse:8123
LO_PLATFORM_URL=http://platform:8002
LO_INTERNAL_ALLOW_HTTP=1
# The Sigma runner's ClickHouse credential. `lo-query` is the user that
# components/data/store-signoz/clickhouse-users.d/lo-query.xml creates and this example's compose
# mounts: readonly=1, allow_ddl=0, SELECT on signoz_traces/signoz_metrics/signoz_logs and nothing
# else, so the runner cannot write to or reshape the store it queries. Point this at a different
# name only if you created that name yourself.
# Scope to be honest about: the store's own `default` user is still passwordless on the project
# network, because SigNoz connects with no credentials (SIGNOZ_TELEMETRYSTORE_CLICKHOUSE_DSN in
# components/data/store-signoz/compose.yaml). Closing that is a store contract change, not an
# example's; components/data/store-signoz/CONTRACT.md records it as the remaining hole.
LO_CLICKHOUSE_USER=lo-query
# Its password, in a one-value file (see the credentials block above):
#   umask 077; printf '%s' "$password" > /approved/deploy/full/private/clickhouse-password
LO_CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD_FILE=
# The same secret a second time, in the one XML form ClickHouse accepts for a user's password: an
# `incl` substitution file. Two envelopes because two readers want two shapes -- the runner presents
# the bare value above as its key, and ClickHouse will not read a bare one-value file as a password.
# examples/full/README.md step 4 generates it FROM that file so the two cannot drift, and chmods it
# like every other file Docker mounts. Required, never defaulted: an unresolved `incl` leaves the
# password element EMPTY, which in ClickHouse means a user with no password, so a missing file must
# stop `config` instead of serving an anonymous reader on the project network.
#   <clickhouse><lo_query_password>the-value</lo_query_password></clickhouse>
LO_CLICKHOUSE_QUERY_CREDENTIALS_FILE=

# query adapter: the analysis reader's ClickHouse user. A SECOND value in a SECOND file: lo-read.xml
# substitutes lo_read_password from it, and an unresolvable substitution is logged and left empty,
# which ClickHouse reads as a user with no password. Same rule as the line above: never a default,
# never optional. Create it with printf, one line, no trailing newline, then wrap it:
#   printf '<clickhouse><lo_read_password>%s</lo_read_password></clickhouse>' "$LO_READ_KEY" > file
# The bare-value twin (LO_CLICKHOUSE_READ_PASSWORD_FILE) is staged in components/control/sigma's own
# block below; the two carry one value in the two forms their readers demand.
LO_CLICKHOUSE_READ_CREDENTIALS_FILE=

# query adapter: the same secret in the bare form lo-read presents as X-ClickHouse-Key. A `_FILE` path, so
# the env-template test requires no `:?` line for it until a manifest names it.
LO_CLICKHOUSE_READ_PASSWORD_FILE=

# The seasonal anomaly producer (local_observe/platform/anomaly.py) is a SERVICE in this example since
# anomaly deployment support: components/control/anomaly/compose.yaml sits in the `include:` list, so the three live lines
# below are required by `config` and are no longer documentation of a process an operator runs by hand.
# The container side of the mount is fixed by that manifest (`LO_ANOMALY_CONFIG=/config/anomaly.json`),
# so what the operator supplies is the host file beside it: a JSON document of series and knobs — up to
# 16 series, each naming its own reviewed SQL and its `sql_sha256` — and a malformed, out-of-range or
# unknown-keyed document is a refusal at load, never a clamp. Stage it readable by uid 65532 BEFORE
# `up`: a host path that does not exist becomes a root-owned directory Docker made for you, which the
# producer then cannot read. The producer's own off switch (no config named -> one INFO line and exit
# 0) is unreachable from this example precisely because the manifest always names it; to run the stack
# without the producer, delete its include entry, which costs anomaly events and nothing else. Knobs,
# bounds and what turning each one down costs: the "Seasonal anomaly baselines" section of
# local_observe/platform/README.md. Its SQL runs as the same read-only `lo-query` user, over the same
# LO_CLICKHOUSE_*, LO_PLATFORM_URL and LO_INTERNAL_ALLOW_HTTP the Sigma runner above uses. Its producer
# credential is its OWN, two lines below: one token cannot authenticate two producer identities.
#   install -m 644 /your/reviewed/anomaly.json /approved/deploy/full/private/anomaly.json
LO_ANOMALY_CONFIG_FILE=

# The producer identity this service stamps on every event it posts, and the cursor records. It must
# equal the `identity` of the producer credential whose token the container presents, because
# `Store.intake` refuses an event whose source is not the authenticated producer — a mismatch is not a
# warning, it is every POST to /v1/events answered `Source identity differs from authenticated
# producer` while the producer keeps judging. Deliberately a DIFFERENT name from
# LO_SIGMA_SOURCE=sigma-example: the store dedups on (source, source_event_id), so two producers
# sharing one identity have their findings folded into one stream, which loses a verdict rather than
# removing a duplicate. The synthetics detector is a THIRD such identity and this file never names it:
# its `source` is the `source:` line of the rule document it evaluates, and its credential is
# `LO_DETECTOR_PRODUCER_TOKEN_FILE` in the synthetics block above.
#   {"identity": "anomaly-example", "role": "producer", "token": "<24+ chars, its own value>"}
LO_ANOMALY_SOURCE=anomaly-example

# The token that identity is authenticated by, in its own file — and it IS a second credential.
# The dedicated anomaly credential asked for that and settled it on 2026-09-10, because one row of the platform's credentials
# file carries exactly one `identity`, so the Sigma runner's `producer-token` file cannot also
# authenticate this producer. Sharing that file was the shipped shape before this line existed, and the
# failure was silent at `up` and loud ever after: the container starts, judges every series, and takes
# `Source identity differs from authenticated producer` on every POST. Nothing is acknowledged, so the
# same window stays owed forever, while the cursor's mtime still moves on every attempt — which is why
# the healthcheck reads green and only the log says `Anomaly round unavailable; the cursor is not
# advanced, this round repeats`. Staging is therefore two steps, not one — a SECOND `producer` row in
# the same JSON file the platform already reads
# (/approved/deploy/full/private/platform-credentials.json, README step 4), whose `identity` is exactly
# the LO_ANOMALY_SOURCE value above, and that row's token copied into its own one-value file beside the
# runner's:
#   umask 077; printf '%s' "<the anomaly-example row's token>" \
#     > /approved/deploy/full/private/anomaly-producer-token
# then `chmod 0444` it with every other mounted file (the loop at the foot of README step 4), because
# the container reads it as uid 65532 and a 0600 file owned by your login is a start-up refusal that
# reads like a bad token. Unset here, `docker compose config` stops and names this variable, which is
# the intended behaviour of a required value. This repository builds no image and starts no container,
# so neither the refusal nor the working path has been run from it.
LO_ANOMALY_PRODUCER_TOKEN_FILE=

# Optional, and read by the container's healthcheck and by nothing else: how long the cursor may sit
# unadvanced before the container reads `unhealthy`. The rule and the one false alarm it admits are in
# components/control/anomaly/CONTRACT.md §6; the shipped default covers the shipped tick and window.
# LO_ANOMALY_STALE_SECONDS=1800

# Inside the `anomaly` service this next value is NOT operator-supplied: the manifest fixes the cursor
# to /state/cursor.json on the `anomaly-state` named volume (which must be prepared before the first
# `up` — components/control/anomaly/CONTRACT.md), so the host form below applies only to a hand-run
# `python -m local_observe.platform.anomaly` process. Both forms describe the same document.
#
# Required whenever a config is named, and not part of that JSON document: the absolute host-local path
# of the anomaly producer's durable cursor (local_observe/platform/anomaly_cursor.py). The main loop runs
# continuously in one process and holds the cursor's exclusive owner lock for that process's whole
# life, so this file is the memory a restart needs, holding which window was judged last, whose turn
# it was, and the evidence and event it still owes; a configured producer that is given no cursor
# refuses to start rather than deliver verdicts it can never re-send. It must be an absolute path with
# no ".." segment and no UNC/device form, on a LOCAL filesystem (the code cannot detect a mapped drive
# or a remote mount; that is the operator's check), inside a private directory that ALREADY exists at
# mode 0700 with no symlinked ancestor -- the producer never creates it, refuses a group- or
# other-reachable parent on POSIX, and refuses a link anywhere above the file -- and the file itself is
# written 0600 with a temp file, fsync and one atomic rename. Losing it is loud but not free: the
# producer re-anchors at a completed window and logs one WARNING that history before that point was
# never judged and is not replayed, and whatever it had not acknowledged is forgotten -- so a window the
# platform already accepted can be judged again, and the store's dedup of identical bytes is not a
# repair for a verdict that came out differently. Restore it together with the platform database, from a
# coherent stopped snapshot, or expect to reconcile by hand; the copy-out and restore commands for the
# volume form are components/control/anomaly/backup.md, and nothing here resets or deletes it. Place it
# inside or outside the host's backup set on purpose.
# LO_ANOMALY_CURSOR=/var/lib/local-observe-anomaly/cursor.json

# Optional, and off by default: the config-snapshot drift producer
# (local_observe/platform/configdrift.py). Set it to a JSON file naming a directory of configuration
# snapshots the operator's own job writes -- one file per <resource_id>/<name>, where resource_id is a
# declared inventory UUID -- plus the artifacts to watch, a cursor path and a round length:
#   {"root": "/approved/snapshots", "cursor": "/approved/deploy/full/private/drift-cursor.json",
#    "resources": [{"resource_id": "<uuid>", "name": "running-config",
#                   "rule_id": "drift.router-running-config"}], "interval_seconds": 300}
# The producer files one `drift` event per artifact whose sha256 moved since it last looked, and a
# `coverage` event when the tree cannot be read; an unchanged file and a file it has never seen both
# produce nothing. No service in this example declares it: run it as `lo-platform drift` for one
# reviewed round into a database, or `python -m local_observe.platform.configdrift` to loop, with
# LO_DRIFT_SOURCE as its producer identity and otherwise the LO_INDEX_PATH, LO_PLATFORM_URL,
# LO_PRODUCER_TOKEN and LO_INTERNAL_ALLOW_HTTP the Sigma runner above already uses. The cursor is the
# producer's own state and holds a copy of each artifact's last text, so keep it mode 0600 and back it
# up with the detector cursors -- it is not in the platform database. Artifacts above 64 KiB are
# refused (reported as coverage, never skipped silently). Unset or blank, it logs one INFO line saying
# it is off and exits 0.
# LO_DRIFT_CONFIG=/approved/deploy/full/private/drift.json
# --- Optional: the AI component, absent by default (component `ai`, AI integration) ---------------------
# AI is optional and excluded from the reference composition. Select a model and
# serving image for the installation, then validate its capability and disclosure
# policy documents before enabling generation. The platform remains usable without it.
# Resolve the digest yourself and pin it: `pull_policy: never` means the daemon will not fetch it at
# boot, so an unresolvable value stops the start rather than substituting something newer.
# LO_AI_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ggml-org/llama.cpp@sha256:<64 hex you resolved>
# Directory holding the GGUF file, created by you, mounted read-only, never downloaded on boot.
# The serve reads it as uid 65532, so it and its parents need read permission for that uid.
#   mkdir -p -m 755 /approved/deploy/full/models
# LO_AI_MODEL_DIR=/approved/deploy/full/models
# The file's name inside that directory.
# LO_AI_MODEL_FILE=qwen3-30b-a3b-q4_k_m.gguf
# The id the API answers to. The serve is started with `--alias` set to this same value, and the
# product client refuses a reply that names a different model, so one line here is the whole pin.
# LO_AI_MODEL=qwen3-30b-a3b
# Optional rerank/summarise slot (model capabilities). Unset, the fast slot uses LO_AI_MODEL.
# LO_AI_MODEL_FAST=qwen3-30b-a3b
# The context the serve is built with. It must equal `context_tokens` in your capability manifest:
# that number is a measurement of this build with these weights on this host, never a figure copied
# from a model card, and every other measurement in that file is worth as much as this one is.
# LO_AI_CTX_SIZE=8192
# Container memory ceiling. Too low and the container is OOM-killed during model load, which reads as
# a broken model; leave it unset and `config` refuses rather than running the host without a bound.
# LO_AI_MEM_LIMIT=12g
# One llama.cpp API key per line (its own format). Generate at least 24 characters, because the
# product's HTTP client refuses a shorter credential rather than sending it:
#   umask 077; printf '%s\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 24)" > /approved/deploy/full/private/ai-api-key
# LO_AI_API_KEY_FILE=/approved/deploy/full/private/ai-api-key
# Where the client (rca, chat) reads its two JSON documents. Copy the shipped starting points out
# first: components/control/ai/capability.example.json has every field "unknown", which is the only
# honest content for a model you have not run yet, and an unmeasured manifest lets NOTHING generate --
# that is the design working, not a bug to route around. policy.example.json refuses `restricted`
# outright and keeps every class inside the LAN.
# LO_AI_CAPABILITY=/approved/deploy/full/private/ai-capability.json
# LO_AI_POLICY=/approved/deploy/full/private/ai-policy.json
# Optional request budget JSON; every limit in it can only make a call smaller. Defaults and ceilings:
# components/control/ai/CONTRACT.md section 6.
# LO_AI_BUDGET=/approved/deploy/full/private/ai-budget.json
# Locality declaration for remote inference policy. Only the exact `0` means "this endpoint is inside the private network and may receive
# a bundle the policy allows". Unset, empty, `true` or a typo are all read as REMOTE, which is the
# safe direction: generation is then refused for any class whose policy entry does not permit a remote
# endpoint. Leave it unset until you have decided where the endpoint actually is.
# LO_AI_OUT_OF_LAN=0
# Payload capture. Off is the default and the only safe setting: `1` puts 300-character excerpts of
# every prompt and response into the container log, which the agent ships into the store's log table
# with the configured log retention (14 days by default), where anyone who can read the
# SigNoz UI can read them. CONTRACT.md section 7 says who can read them and why the caps overwrite
# them within minutes.
# LO_AI_CAPTURE=1
# The client reaches the serve over the project network at http://ai:8080, which is plaintext: it is
# accepted only while LO_INTERNAL_ALLOW_HTTP=1, which this example already sets for the Sigma runner.
# Nothing here publishes a host port; see CONTRACT.md section 8 for why the loopback option was not
# taken.

# --- Optional: the chat surface, absent by default (component `chat`, chat integration) -------------------
# Chat is optional and excluded from the reference composition. Read its contract
# before adding the manifest to the existing include list (README step 10).
# Configure a per-agent MCP endpoint and validate the integration end to end.
# Chat cannot approve or execute platform actions. Runtime acceptance is not-run.
# Resolve the digest yourself against the registry you pull from; components/control/chat/versions.json
# records the one this row read on 2026-09-09, its 1,101,725,314-byte download, and the AVX2 check that
# must pass before the first start. `pull_policy: never` means an unresolvable value stops the boot.
# LO_CHAT_IMAGE=docker.io/mintplexlabs/anythingllm@sha256:<64 hex you resolved>
# Container memory ceiling. Upstream's own stated minimum for this container is 2 GB -- their figure,
# not a measurement taken here -- on a host whose published floor (hardware baseline) is 16 GB for `minimal` while
# this example is `standard` and already runs the store, the platform, Dagu, Healthchecks, the Sigma
# runner, the synthetics pair and the portal. Set it knowingly or do not opt in.
# LO_CHAT_MEM_LIMIT=2g
# Optional CPU cap (default 2, upstream's stated minimum) and the bound on one agent turn's chained
# tool calls (default 10, upstream's own documented example value).
# LO_CHAT_CPUS=2
# LO_CHAT_MAX_TOOL_CALLS=10
# AnythingLLM's own settings file: SERVER_PORT, JWT_SECRET/SIG_KEY/SIG_SALT, AUTH_TOKEN and any
# provider key, in plaintext, mounted read-only at /app/server/.env -- the path the pinned build's
# dotenv reads, and the only way a credential in this product's shape arrives as a file rather than a
# `docker inspect`-able environment value. Create it and own it so uid 1000 (the image's user) can read
# the mount; Compose preserves the host ownership and mode, it does not copy the file:
#   umask 077; printf 'STORAGE_DIR="/app/server/storage"\n' > /approved/deploy/full/private/chat.env
#   chown 1000:1000 /approved/deploy/full/private/chat.env && chmod 0444 /approved/deploy/full/private/chat.env
# Accept two consequences before you write it, both in CONTRACT.md sections 3 and 7: the pinned build
# rewrites this file's content on a UI action (POST /api/system/update-env), which a read-only mount
# refuses, so the onboarding wizard's provider-key save fails here loudly; and anything you put in it
# persists on the host outside every retention rule this product enforces.
# LO_CHAT_SETTINGS_FILE=/approved/deploy/full/private/chat.env
# No Telegram line exists in this block and none will be added: the row's Local MVP role is a
# non-Telegram workflow (integration validation), and the surface's own Telegram connector ships unconfigured and stays
# that way (CONTRACT.md section 6). The scripts that use this product's send-only Telegram client are
# private rehearsal tooling and are not a documented step.
# Not enabled by this repository: the loopback publication. components/control/chat/compose.yaml
# carries the intended `ports:` line as a comment with the two-file widening it needs
# (HOST_PUBLISHED_SERVICES plus the tuple pinned in tests/test_ai_component.py); 18101 is reserved for
# it here because 18100 is the MCP tool surface (component `mcp`, the block below), 18097 is the portal, 18099 is Healthchecks and 18098 is that component's restore
# scratch project.

# --- Optional: the MCP tool surface, absent by default (component `mcp`, MCP component) ----------------
# Every line in this block is commented out on purpose, and this example's compose.yaml has no `mcp`
# service to include: the store, the platform, the operator UI, the portal and every /v1/* route boot,
# page, approve and record with no MCP image anywhere on the host. Opting in is ONE line added to the
# `include:` list in examples/full/compose.yaml, plus this block (README.md, step 9) — and it is a
# decision to let an agent read your platform state and file approval requests under its own name.
# First-party image: build it (`docs/BUILD.md`) and paste the local config ID that build printed. This
# repository publishes nothing, and `pull_policy: never` means the daemon will not fetch a value you
# guessed at — the manifest sets no tag to fall back on.
#   docker build -f components/control/mcp/Dockerfile \
#     --build-arg LO_SOURCE_REVISION="$(git rev-parse HEAD)" -t local-observe-mcp:dev .
#   docker image inspect local-observe-mcp:dev --format '{{.Id}}'      # -> sha256:<64 hex>
# LO_MCP_IMAGE=sha256:<the config ID that build printed>
# The loopback host port, 18100 because no shipped example uses it (demo 18081/13133/14317/14318, this
# example 18084 + 18091-18097 + 18099, the platform stage 18083) and 18098 stays free for the restore
# scratch project the component backup/upgrade documents use. It is published at all — the only optional
# component here that is — because an MCP client is a process on ANOTHER host and cannot join this
# project's network; anything past the Docker host is the TLS + authentication edge that
# components/control/mcp/CONTRACT.md §6 names and this repository does not start.
# LO_MCP_PORT=18100
# The per-agent credential map, if you have not already set it above in the credentials block. One
# variable, one file: the MCP container mounts the SAME read-only policy directory the platform does, so
# the map staged beside actions.json is the one it reads and a rotation touches one file, not two.
# LO_MCP_IDENTITIES=/config/mcp-identities.json
# Optional store read — it is what turns the `signal_series` tool ON. Blank, `open_reader()` logs one
# line and the tool is absent from the advertised list rather than present and failing. The endpoint is
# the Sigma runner's variable, already set above; this is only the credential, named as a CONTAINER path
# inside /config so it can stay optional (a Compose `secrets:` entry cannot be conditional, and a
# required one would stop the install that runs no store read). Stage it with printf, no trailing
# newline, then chmod 0444 so uid 65532 can read the mount:
#   install -m 644 /approved/private/clickhouse-read-password \
#     /approved/deploy/full/policy/clickhouse-read-password
# LO_MCP_READ_PASSWORD_PATH=/config/clickhouse-read-password
# The read-only user the series read authenticates as; `lo-read` is the account
# components/data/store-signoz/clickhouse-users.d/lo-read.xml creates, so this is a name and not a
# secret, and the password above stays required the moment the read is turned on.
# LO_CLICKHOUSE_READ_USER=lo-read
# The declared-graph read (`topology_neighbourhood`). Defaulted to the snapshot the /inventory bind
# carries, which this example mounts anyway — blank this variable if you mount nothing there, because
# the tool is advertised when an index is configured and every walk then fails on a missing file.
# LO_MCP_INDEX_PATH=/inventory/inventory.db

# --- Published ports (loopback only) ---------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately different from examples/demo, so both examples can coexist on one host.
LO_UI_PORT=18091
LO_OTLP_GRPC_PORT=18092
LO_OTLP_HTTP_PORT=18093
LO_INGEST_HEALTH_PORT=18094
LO_INVENTORY_PORT=18095
# One port serves both the role API and the operator UI (same container, same origin).
LO_PLATFORM_PORT=18096
# The Dagu UI. Kept at 18084, the number this component published before the variable existed, so
# adding the variable changed no operator-visible address; change it here if another stack holds it.
LO_DAGU_PORT=18084
# The operator portal's own page. Loopback like every other publication here; anything beyond the
# Docker host goes through the TLS and authentication edge recipe in
# components/control/homepage/CONTRACT.md, which this repository ships as a recipe and never starts.
LO_HOMEPAGE_PORT=18097
# Homepage refuses a request whose Host header is not on this list, so this must name the host:port
# the browser actually uses -- with the loopback publication above, that is 127.0.0.1:18097 (comma-
# separate more names if you browse by DNS name or through the TLS edge). Required, not defaulted: it
# has to agree with LO_HOMEPAGE_PORT by hand, and Compose cannot interpolate one variable into another.
LO_HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=127.0.0.1:18097
# The Healthchecks console: the list of checks, their deadlines, and the operator's own read-only
# API key for the scrape endpoint. Loopback like everything else here, which is also why a job on
# ANOTHER host cannot check in to it -- see components/control/job-observe/CONTRACT.md "Limits of the
# loopback-only rule". The manifest's own default is 18097; this example moves it to 18099 because
# operator portal gave 18097 to the portal, and leaves 18098 free since that component's backup.md and
# upgrade.md use 18098 for the restore scratch project, whose port must differ from the live one.
# Change the port and you must change the three lines below, which are the URLs generated ping lines
# and the Host header the app accepts; a SITE_ROOT whose host is missing from ALLOWED_HOSTS is a
# container that never becomes healthy (upstream system check hc.api.E002).
LO_HEALTHCHECKS_PORT=18099
LO_HEALTHCHECKS_SITE_ROOT=http://127.0.0.1:18099
LO_HEALTHCHECKS_ALLOWED_HOSTS=127.0.0.1,localhost
LO_HEALTHCHECKS_PING_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:18099/ping/

# --- Optional settings -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Delivery posture. recording (the default) stores notification attempts and sends nothing;
# off disables the outbox; live actually sends and is refused by these manifests' default.
LO_NOTIFICATION_MODE=recording
# Optional JSON notification-safety policy (budgets, synthetic sources, test window).
LO_NOTIFICATION_POLICY=
# Optional label/display mapping applied when formatting human-facing messages. In live mode it may
# carry "callback_base": one HTTPS origin (no credentials, no query, no fragment) that the alert
# message names as the place to send back its single-use approval code. Without it the message says
# nothing about approvals, which is the safe answer: a wrong or truncated destination is worse than
# none, because an operator will trust whichever one they are shown.
LO_DISPLAY_CONFIG=
# Optional observation document summarised by GET /v1/overview.
LO_OVERVIEW_PATH=
# Webhook channel, only honoured in live mode. Leave LO_NOTIFY_URL empty to keep delivery off.
LO_NOTIFY_URL=
# Its credential has no line here on purpose: it is the file /config/notify-token inside the
# container, which is the directory LO_PLATFORM_POLICY_DIR already mounts read-only, so write it
# beside actions.json when you do turn on live delivery (README.md, step 5). A Compose secret
# cannot be declared conditionally, and a required secret would stop a recording-mode install that
# has no channel to configure at all.
LO_NOTIFY_ALLOW_HTTP=0
# Telegram channel config JSON path (token, chat_id); honoured in live mode only.
LO_TELEGRAM_CONFIG=
# More than one channel: a mounted JSON document listing them in preference order, each entry
#   {"channel": <label>, "type": "telegram", "config": <path>}   or
#   {"channel": <label>, "type": "webhook", "url": <https>, "token_file": <path>, "policy": {...}}
# and since delivery channels the five transports ported from v0.1's notifier set, same document, same rules:
#   {"channel": "push",  "type": "ntfy",       "url": "https://ntfy.example", "topic": "ops-alerts",
#                                              "token_file": "<path>"}
#   {"channel": "chat",  "type": "slack",      "url_file": "<path holding the whole webhook URL>"}
#   {"channel": "room",  "type": "matrix",     "url": "https://matrix.example",
#                                              "room_id": "!alerts:example.org", "token_file": "<path>"}
#   {"channel": "paged", "type": "pagerduty",  "key_file": "<path holding the Events v2 routing key>"}
#   {"channel": "mail",  "type": "email",      "host": "smtp.example", "port": 587,
#                       "from": "lo@example.org", "recipients_file": "<path, one address a line>",
#                       "username": "lo", "password_file": "<path>"}
# Read in live mode only, so recording/off installs never open it. No credential is ever an environment
# value here: a channel's secret is a file whose path sits inside this document, which is why no
# LO_CHANNEL_* variable exists and none needs to. An email channel cannot send a password to a relay
# that has not upgraded, so STARTTLS is on and cannot be turned off where a password_file is named.
# ITSM is not one of these types and never will be (itop). No commented variable here holds a value:
# every one of these paths is staged in the directory below, not typed into this file.
# LO_NOTIFY_URL and LO_TELEGRAM_CONFIG above remain the single-channel spelling and still refuse
# each other; a document entry that names a channel one of them already claimed fails the boot.
# The value is a container path, so the document is staged in LO_PLATFORM_POLICY_DIR (the directory
# already mounted read-only at /config) and named from inside it, beside actions.json; the manifest
# adds no mount for it and no token of its own. Empty keeps the registry off. README.md, step 5.
LO_NOTIFY_CHANNELS=
# Collector-to-collector endpoints; the defaults name the services in this project.
LO_INGEST_URL=http://lo-front-door:4318
LO_STORE_OTLP_ENDPOINT=signoz-otel-collector:4317

# --- Telemetry retention -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# How long the store keeps each signal. SigNoz retention is an API setting rather than a file a
# container could read, so nothing here reaches the store by itself and no service applies retention
# when it boots. These are the numbers you intend; the tool that writes them through the SigNoz API
# and reads them back is run by you, once per store bring-up, restore and upgrade:
#   set -a; . /approved/private/full.env; set +a
#   python3 components/data/store-signoz/retention.py --url "http://127.0.0.1:$LO_UI_PORT" \
#     --credentials-file /approved/private/signoz-login.json \
#     --traces-days "$LO_RETENTION_TRACES_DAYS" --metrics-days "$LO_RETENTION_METRICS_DAYS" \
#     --logs-days "$LO_RETENTION_LOGS_DAYS"
# It prints one line per signal and exits 0 when all three already match, 1 when any differs (add
# --apply to set them; --apply then re-reads, so an ignored write is not reported as success) and 2
# when the setting cannot be read at all. Until that command has run against a fresh store, the
# store keeps SigNoz's own defaults, not these. Mechanism, the login file, the API paths and the
# irreversibility of shortening a TTL: components/data/store-signoz/CONTRACT.md.
# Commented out on purpose: this template carries only lines a manifest reads (a test enforces it),
# and no manifest can read a value that must be POSTed to an API -- wiring the apply into bring-up
# is the open follow-up recorded against retention.
#
# Disk maths -- measure, never estimate. Retention is the only bound on how large clickhouse-data
# grows, and per signal:
#   bytes_kept = ingested_bytes_per_day_per_signal * compression_factor * RETENTION_DAYS
# The volume must hold that SUMMED over traces+metrics+logs plus the headroom one partition merge
# needs. Both ingested_bytes_per_day_per_signal and compression_factor belong to YOUR workload and
# the pinned build, so there is no honest number to ship here: ingest a known volume over a known
# window, take `du -s` of the clickhouse-data volume, and re-measure after any collector or SigNoz
# upgrade, because a schema change moves both terms.
#LO_RETENTION_TRACES_DAYS=7
#LO_RETENTION_METRICS_DAYS=30
#LO_RETENTION_LOGS_DAYS=14
